# q35-linux — the q35 chipset with a kernel in the socket instead of a firmware.
#
#   rsemu run q35-linux --media kernel=bzImage
#   rsemu run q35-linux --media kernel=bzImage --media initrd=initramfs.cpio \
#                       --drive nvme0=disk.img
#
# # Why this board exists, and why it is not `q35` with a flag
#
# Two boards each had half of what a kernel needs.
#
# `machines/q35.machine` has the chipset — ECAM, the PAM file at 0x90, the
# ICH9's `PIRQ` routers, a generated set of ACPI tables — and therefore a PCI
# bus with somewhere to put a disk controller. What it has is a **firmware**
# socket at `0xffff0000`, and what runs on it starts in real mode at
# `0xfffffff0`.
#
# `machines/pc64.machine` has the other half: `x86.linuxboot` writes a
# `bzImage` into memory and enters it at its own 32-bit entry point, which is
# the one interface a Linux kernel offers to something that is not a BIOS. What
# it has *not* got is a PCI bus, so the only root a kernel on it can be given
# is an initramfs.
#
# The two cannot be one board. `x86.linuxboot` maps its reset stub at the top
# of the address space, which is exactly where the firmware socket is; a board
# with both would have two things decoding `0xfffffff0`. So this is the third
# board: **`q35`'s chipset, `pc64`'s entry, and an NVM Express controller on
# the bus for the kernel to find a disk on.**
#
# Everything below the chipset section is the object `machines/q35.machine`
# uses at the address it uses, and the two files should be read side by side —
# this one documents the *differences* rather than re-describing an ICH9.
#
# # What is here that `q35` has not got
#
#   * **`x86.linuxboot`** instead of `pc.rom`. `src/dev/linuxboot.rs` has the
#     protocol; `docs/platforms/pc64.md` has what a kernel entered that way
#     does before it prints its first character.
#   * **`nvme.controller`** on the PCI bus at device 4. Why NVMe and not the
#     three other controllers this tree has is the one real design decision on
#     this board, and it is measured rather than preferred — see below.
#
# # What is here that `q35` has and this does not
#
#   * **No firmware, no video adapter and no option ROM.** The console is the
#     16550, because that is what `console=ttyS0` names and because a board
#     with no BIOS has no `INT 10h` to set a text mode with. `x86.linuxboot`
#     leaves `screen_info` in the zero page zeroed, which is how the kernel is
#     told there is no video console.
#   * **No 8042, and A20 is deliberately unwired.** A net with a driver comes
#     up low and a low `a20` pin shuts the gate; on `q35` the firmware opens it
#     during POST and here there is no firmware to. An unwired pin is a
#     permanently connected address line, which is the machine we want — a
#     kernel entered in protected mode never asks for anything else. The same
#     reasoning `machines/pc64.machine` writes out at its own `wire` block.
#   * **No IDE and no 8237As.** Each would be a second thing being tested, and
#     the kernel this board is for does not build a driver for either.
#
# # Why NVMe
#
# Not because it is the newest. Because of what a *stock distribution kernel*
# has built in, which is the constraint that decides this and not which
# controller is easiest to wire.
#
# A distribution kernel builds nearly every storage driver as a **module**, and
# a module lives on the root that has not been mounted yet. Measured against a
# stock Gentoo 6.6.67 build's own `modules.builtin` — which is the kernel
# saying what it linked in, not a guess:
#
#   * `drivers/nvme/host/nvme.ko` is **built in**. It is also self-contained:
#     an NVMe namespace becomes `/dev/nvme0n1` through blk-mq with no SCSI
#     layer under it.
#   * `drivers/ata/libata.ko` is built in but `libahci`/`ahci` are not, and
#     `sd_mod` is not either — so an AHCI adapter on this board would be
#     enumerated and then have no driver to bind, and even with one there would
#     be no SCSI disk to publish the block device.
#   * There is no virtio of any kind in that list.
#
# So NVMe is the one controller a kernel that has never heard of rsemu can
# actually drive off a board like this. A kernel with a different set built in
# is a different answer, and this file is one `object` and one `wire` away from
# either of the others — `machines/ahci-mini.machine` and
# `tests/riscv_virtio_blk.rs` show both shapes.
#
# # The interrupt the disk arrives on
#
# Nothing is wired for it, and that is the point. The controller drives its own
# `INTA#`; `src/bus/pci` rotates it by device number onto one of the bus's four
# interrupt nets; the ICH9 collects those nets as `PIRQ[A-D]` because it says
# `bus = "pci0"`; the `pirq-routes` below say where a POST would have steered
# them; and `q35.acpi`'s `_PRT` is generated from the routers the bridge is
# actually holding, so what the kernel reads out of the DSDT and what the
# hardware does cannot disagree. Device 4 with `INTA#` swizzles to `PIRQ[A]`,
# which these routes put on IRQ11.
#
# # Sources
#
# `machines/q35.machine`'s, unchanged — the Intel 3 Series datasheet
# (316966-002), the ICH9 datasheet (316972-004), ACPI 6.5 — plus the NVM
# Express specification for the controller and the PC16550D data sheet for the
# UART. The kernel image is *data*: it is loaded and run, never read as source
# (`ROADMAP.md` §1).

machine "q35-linux" {
  # -- parameters -------------------------------------------------------------

  # Base memory: the 640 KiB below the video hole, which is where the loader
  # puts the zero page, the command line and its mode-switch stub.
  param basemem = 640K

  # Extended memory, from 0x100000 up. A current kernel wants `init_size` —
  # around 54 MiB for a distribution build — clear above its load address
  # before it can decompress, an initramfs on top of that, and then a page
  # cache to read a disk into. 256 MiB is what `pc64` settled on and it is what
  # the e820 map below describes; the machine refuses to build if the kernel
  # bound to it does not fit.
  param extmem = 256M

  # The kernel command line.
  #
  # `nokaslr` is not a preference and it is the same line `pc64` carries, for
  # the same reason: address-space randomisation draws entropy partly from the
  # 8254, by issuing a read-back command and spinning until the **null-count**
  # bit in the status byte goes clear. That bit is set by a control-word write
  # and cleared when a count is actually loaded (Intel 8254 data sheet,
  # "Read-Back Command"), so on a machine where nothing has ever programmed
  # counter 0 it never clears. On `q35` a firmware programs it during POST and
  # the bit is measurably clear by the time a guest runs; **this board has no
  # firmware**, so it is back, and the line has to be here. Determinism wants
  # it anyway (CLAUDE.md).
  #
  # There is **nothing else here**, and that is the measurement worth keeping:
  # no `nolapic`, no `noapic`, no `hpet=disable`. On this line a current kernel
  # takes the symmetric I/O path — the MADT read, the IMCR switched, the I/O
  # APIC programmed, and the tick arriving from the HPET through its legacy
  # replacement route — instead of panicking in `check_timer()` the way it did
  # while nothing drove IRQ0. Each of those three words was needed at some
  # point and none is now; `docs/platforms/q35-linux.md` says what each was
  # hiding and what fixed it, and where the boot stops today.
  param cmdline = "console=ttyS0,115200 earlyprintk=ttyS0,115200 nokaslr"

  # Where the ACPI tables are staged. ACPI §5.2.5.1 has an operating system
  # search "the BIOS read-only memory space between 0E0000h and 0FFFFFh" on
  # 16-byte boundaries for the RSDP, and this is the low edge of it. On `q35`
  # this address is shared with a firmware socket that begins at 0xf0000; here
  # there is no socket, and the 64 KiB from 0xf0000 up decodes nothing.
  param acpibase = 0xe0000
  param acpisize = 64K

  # Where `PCIEXBAR` comes out of reset pointing, with its enable bit set, and
  # where `PMBASE` does. Both are firmware stand-ins on `q35` and both are
  # *load bearing* here, because there is no firmware at all to program them:
  # a kernel that found `PCIEXBAR` disabled would fall back to 0xcf8, and one
  # that found `PMBASE` unplaced would have an FADT naming an ACPI register
  # block that does not decode. §5.1.16 and ICH9 §13.1.13-§13.1.14 give the
  # registers; the values are this board's.
  param ecambase = 0xe0000000
  param pmbase   = 0x600

  # Which host character port COM1 attaches to, so two of these can run in one
  # process without writing over each other.
  param console = "console"

  # How big the NVMe namespace is when the run supplies no image. A medium
  # installed under the `nvme0` slot (`--drive nvme0=disk.img`) wins over this
  # and brings its own capacity.
  param disk = 16M

  # Bytes in a logical block. 512 is what every driver assumes without asking;
  # 4096 is what a modern part reports, and this board can be either.
  param block = 512

  # The date and time the RTC starts at. A device may never read the host clock
  # (CLAUDE.md, determinism), so it is declared.
  param time = "2026-01-01T00:00:00"

  # Which execution engine the processor runs on: `interp`, `jit`, or
  # `jit-host`.
  #
  #   rsemu run q35-linux -p engine=jit
  #
  # A parameter rather than a fixed property for the reason
  # `machines/riscv-virt.machine` gives at length: the three are supposed to
  # be *indistinguishable to the guest* — same registers, same memory, same
  # cycle counts — and the only way to keep that claim honest is for one
  # board to run all three and have the state hashes compared. `tests/x86_engines.rs`
  # does that; `docs/platforms/q35-linux.md` has what each one costs.
  #
  # `interp` is the default because it is the oracle. Both others need a
  # build with `cpu-x86-lift` and `jit`, and a build without them refuses
  # the property with a message rather than quietly interpreting.
  param engine = "interp"

  # -- crystals ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `q35`'s, less the two that only a video adapter and an 8042 counted.

  # The processor. 100 MHz rather than `q35`'s 25, and the number is a
  # measurement rather than a part: a kernel boot is a fixed amount of *work*,
  # and a slower core spreads it over more virtual time — more timer
  # interrupts, more `udelay` spins charged against a slower clock, and more
  # wall clock for the same boot. `pc64` settled on 100 MHz for exactly this
  # and this board is doing the same job.
  osc cpu   = 100000000 Hz
  osc pit   = 105000000/88 Hz
  osc rtc   = 32768 Hz
  osc bus   = 100000000 Hz
  osc hpet  = 10000000 Hz

  # The ACPI power-management timer: 14.31818 MHz divided by four, written as
  # the division because neither number is an integer count of hertz and the
  # language takes rational literals so the ratio stays exact (ICH9 §13.8.3.4).
  osc pmtmr = 315000000/88 Hz

  # The UART's. 1.8432 MHz divided by sixteen is one character time at the
  # fastest rate the chip runs.
  osc uart  = 1843200 Hz

  # -- address spaces ---------------------------------------------------------
  space mem  { width = 64, unassigned = read-as-ones }
  space port { width = 16, unassigned = read-as-ones }

  # -- the processor ----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `variant = "x86-64"` is a preset (ROADMAP.md §6.1.1): the point in the
  # extension lattice every part that implements long mode has to be at. The
  # kernel's decompressor builds page tables, sets `EFER.LME`, enters long mode
  # and decompresses itself before printing a character, so anything that
  # reaches the first line of output has already exercised most of it.
  object cpu0 "cpu.x86" {
    clock   = cpu
    space   = mem
    iospace = "port"
    variant = "x86-64"
    engine  = engine
  }

  # -- memory -----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Declared before anything that writes into memory: `Machine::reset` runs
  # devices in declaration order and a cold reset zeroes RAM, so the loader at
  # the bottom of this file must run after these.
  object ram_low  "ram" { size = basemem }
  object ram_high "ram" { size = extmem }

  # -- the north bridge -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `machines/q35.machine`'s, unchanged and for its reasons: `device-id =
  # 0x29c0` is the identification the world recognises for this part family
  # (datasheet §5.1.2, Table 5-1), `clock = bus` is there because a
  # configuration write through ECAM can only be applied at a scheduler tick,
  # and `passthrough = sysctl` hands the byte at 0xcf9 to the south bridge's
  # reset control register.
  object mch "q35.mch" {
    device-id   = 0x29c0
    clock       = bus
    space       = mem
    bus         = "pci0"
    ecam        = ecambase
    passthrough = sysctl
  }

  # -- the south bridge -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `machines/q35.machine`'s, with one property added. `device-id` is required
  # and has no datasheet value — §13.1.2 defers it to a Specification Update
  # that could not be retrieved — so 0x2918 is this board's claim, exactly as
  # it is there.
  #
  # `sci-en = true` is the addition, and it is the same *kind* of thing
  # `pirq-routes` is: a stand-in for a POST this board does not have. An ICH9
  # powers up with `PM1_CNT.SCI_EN` clear, meaning an enabled ACPI event would
  # raise `SMI#` rather than `SCI` (§13.8.3.3), and firmware sets the bit when
  # the operating system asks for ownership through the SMI command port. This
  # board has no SMI path and no firmware to answer on one, and `q35.acpi`
  # therefore publishes an FADT with `SMI_CMD` zero — which ACPI §5.2.9 defines
  # as "this system does not support ACPI mode transitions". An operating
  # system that reads that and then finds `SCI_EN` clear has been told two
  # contradictory things: there is no way into ACPI mode, and we are not in it.
  # So the board states the bit a firmware would have set, and the two agree.
  object lpc "q35.lpc" {
    clock       = pmtmr
    bus         = "pci0"
    iospace     = "port"
    device-id   = 0x2918
    pm-base     = pmbase
    sci-en      = true
    pirq-routes = [11, 10, 11, 10]
  }

  # -- the ACPI tables --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Generated at reset from the realized machine, exactly as on `q35`, and this
  # is the answer to "who publishes ACPI on a board with no firmware": nobody
  # has to. The tables are a device with a region, the machine file maps that
  # region inside §5.2.5.1's own RSDP search window, and a kernel scanning
  # 0xe0000-0xfffff finds them there without anything having staged them. On a
  # real machine the firmware would allocate this and report it in e820 as ACPI
  # reclaim memory; `docs/platforms/q35.md` says why that is where this should
  # end up.
  #
  # The three declared properties are the three that cannot be read out of the
  # topology: how many processors there are, the I/O APIC's ID (reaching it
  # means writing its index register, which a debug read may not do), and which
  # interrupt the SCI appears on.
  object acpi "q35.acpi" {
    space      = mem
    iospace    = "port"
    bus        = "pci0"
    size       = acpisize
    cpus       = 1
    ioapic-id  = 1
    sci-irq    = 9
  }

  # -- interrupts -------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `q35`'s, unchanged: two 8259As with the slave cascaded onto the master's
  # IR2, a local APIC, an I/O APIC, and the MP specification's IMCR deciding
  # which path the master's INT takes. The kernel switches the IMCR itself once
  # it has read the MADT and seen `PCAT_COMPAT`.
  object pic1   "pc.pic"    { mode = "master" }
  object pic2   "pc.pic"    { mode = "slave" }
  object lapic0 "pc.lapic"  { clock = bus, id = 0, bus = "apic" }
  object ioapic "pc.ioapic" { id = 1, bus = "apic" }
  object imcr   "pc.imcr"   {}

  # -- the timers -------------------------------------------------------------

  object pit0 "pc.pit" { clock = pit }

  # The HPET's three comparators on I/O APIC inputs 16, 17 and 18, off the
  # inputs the SCI can be steered onto. `q35` explains the choice. Comparators
  # 0 and 1 leave those inputs for IRQ0 and IRQ8 while the legacy replacement
  # route is set, which is the block after this one.
  object hpet0 "pc.hpet" {
    clock  = hpet
    period = 100000000
    route0 = 16
    route1 = 17
    route2 = 18
  }

  # -- the legacy replacement route -------------------------------------------
  #
  # The gate the IA-PC HPET specification §2.3.5 describes, built out of the
  # combinators `ROADMAP.md` §4.3 ships. `LEG_RT_CNF` is not a register in any
  # one chip: it is a **board-level multiplexer** between three of them, and
  # the specification says exactly what it selects.
  #
  #   | `LEG_RT_CNF` | IRQ0 / I/O APIC input 2 | IRQ8 / I/O APIC input 8 |
  #   | --- | --- | --- |
  #   | clear | the 8254's counter 0     | the RTC's periodic interrupt |
  #   | set   | the HPET's comparator 0  | the HPET's comparator 1      |
  #
  # and while it is set comparators 0 and 1 stop driving the inputs their
  # `Tn_INT_ROUTE_CNF` names — which is why there are five gates here and not
  # three. `pc.hpet` reports the bit on its `legacy` output pin (it may not
  # reach across a pin and disconnect a sibling), announced from state on the
  # realize sweep and after a restore, and everything below is the board.
  #
  # **This is load bearing on this board and not decoration.** Linux's
  # `hpet_time_init()` programs the 8254 *only if* `hpet_enable()` fails; when
  # it succeeds it sets `LEG_RT_CNF` and expects comparator 0 on IRQ0 instead.
  # Without these gates counter 0 is never loaded and nothing at all drives
  # IRQ0, so `check_timer()` finds no tick on I/O APIC input 2 and panics with
  # `IO-APIC + timer doesn't work!` — and its `8254 timer not connected to
  # IO-APIC` was literally true. `docs/platforms/q35-linux.md` has the log.
  object legoff "wire.not" {}

  # The two chips the HPET displaces, each held on its line only while the bit
  # is clear.
  object pitgate "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }
  object rtcgate "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }

  # The two comparators that take those lines over while it is set.
  object hpetir0 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }
  object hpetir8 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }

  # And comparator 0 and 1 on their own routes, which they abandon while it is
  # set. Comparator 2 keeps its route unconditionally and needs no gate.
  object hpetr0 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }
  object hpetr1 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }

  # -- the real-time clock and CMOS -------------------------------------------
  #
  # The memory sizes are written here as well as being the `ram` objects' sizes
  # because a device cannot read the machine's memory map. Note that a kernel
  # entered by `x86.linuxboot` believes the **e820 map** rather than CMOS; this
  # is here for the wall clock, and the sizes are declared so the two cannot
  # disagree if something does read them.
  object cmos "pc.rtc" {
    clock   = rtc
    time    = time
    basemem = basemem
    extmem  = extmem
  }

  # -- the system control ports -----------------------------------------------
  #
  # Not a chip: latches and gates on the board. Port B at 0x61 gates counter 2
  # and reads its output back, which is the path `quick_pit_calibrate` takes.
  object sysctl "pc.sysctl" {}

  # -- the console ------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A 16550 at 0x3f8 on IRQ4. On a real q35 it is a Super I/O part on the LPC
  # bus that the south bridge decodes for (ICH9 §13.1.3, §13.1.4); this board
  # models the chip and not the decode, because the register file a guest
  # touches is the 16550's either way.
  object com1 "uart.ns16550" {
    clock     = uart / 16
    port      = console
    frequency = 1843200
  }

  # -- the disk ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # An NVM Express controller at device 4, function 0. `space = mem` is not
  # decoration: it is a **bus master**, and its submission queues, its
  # completion queues, its PRP lists and its data buffers are all in this
  # board's RAM, which it reads and writes itself. Without the property it has
  # nowhere to fetch a command from and `bind` says so.
  #
  # Its register block is **not** in the memory map below, and that is the
  # point of a base address register: the kernel sizes `BAR0` and places the
  # block wherever its own resource allocator decides.
  object nvme0 "nvme.controller" {
    space  = mem
    bus    = "pci0"
    device = 4
    image  = "nvme0"
    size   = disk
    block  = block
  }

  # -- what gets loaded -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Last, and that is load bearing: a cold reset zeroes RAM and anything
  # written before that reset would be erased by it.
  #
  # `extmem` is repeated here because this is what builds the e820 map, and a
  # kernel believes the map rather than the machine. One parameter, so the two
  # cannot disagree.
  object boot "x86.linuxboot" {
    space   = mem
    kernel  = "kernel"
    initrd  = "initrd"
    cmdline = cmdline
    basemem = basemem
    extmem  = extmem
  }

  # -- the memory map ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # 0x00000    base RAM, up to the video hole
  # 0xe0000    the ACPI tables
  # 0x100000   extended memory
  # 0xfec00000 the I/O APIC, 0xfed00000 the HPET, 0xfee00000 the local APIC
  # 0xfffffff0 the loader's reset vector
  #
  # The ECAM window is not here — `PCIEXBAR` places it — and neither is the
  # NVMe controller's register block, which `BAR0` places.
  map mem 0x00000000 size basemem  = ram_low
  map mem acpibase   size acpisize = acpi.tables
  map mem 0x00100000 size extmem   = ram_high

  map mem 0xfec00000 size 0x0020   = ioapic.regs   # 82093AA §3.1
  map mem 0xfed00000 size 0x0400   = hpet0.regs    # HPET spec §3.2.4
  map mem 0xfee00000 size 0x1000   = lapic0.regs   # SDM Vol 3A §10.4.4

  # The sixteen bytes at the top of the address space, and the only thing on
  # this board above 4 GiB minus a page. A processor fetches its first
  # instruction from 0xfffffff0 with CS's cached base still at 0xffff0000; what
  # is there is a far jump to the loader's stub in low memory, which is the one
  # instruction that can change that base.
  map mem 0x100000000 - 16 size 16 = boot.reset

  # -- the I/O map ------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The AT's, less everything this board does not have. The ACPI register block
  # is not here: `PMBASE` places it, exactly as `PCIEXBAR` places ECAM.
  map port 0x0020 size 0x0002 = pic1.regs     # master 8259A
  map port 0x0022 size 0x0002 = imcr.regs     # the MP specification's IMCR
  map port 0x0040 size 0x0004 = pit0.regs     # 8254
  map port 0x0061 size 0x0001 = sysctl.portb  # speaker, timer 2 out, refresh
  map port 0x0070 size 0x0002 = cmos.regs     # RTC index (with the NMI mask)
  map port 0x0092 size 0x0001 = sysctl.porta  # fast A20 and fast reset
  map port 0x00a0 size 0x0002 = pic2.regs     # slave 8259A
  map port 0x03f8 size 0x0008 = com1.regs     # COM1
  map port 0x04d0 size 0x0001 = pic1.elcr     # edge/level control, master
  map port 0x04d1 size 0x0001 = pic2.elcr     # edge/level control, slave

  # PCI configuration mechanism #1, and the reset control register at 0xcf9
  # inside it, handed on to `sysctl` through the north bridge's pass-through.
  # ECAM does not replace this window — a q35 has both, and Linux probes here
  # first and then upgrades to the window `MCFG` names.
  map port 0x0cf8 size 0x0008 = mch.config

  # -- the wires --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The interrupt tree, `q35`'s: the slave cascades onto the master's IR2, the
  # master's INT reaches the processor through the IMCR, and the local APIC's
  # own output is wire-ORed onto the same pin. The order of the two `cpu0.intr`
  # drivers is load bearing — the IMCR is asked for an acknowledge first and
  # declines when the register says the APIC owns the pin.
  wire pic2.int    -> pic1.ir2
  wire pic1.int    -> imcr.int
  wire imcr.intr   -> cpu0.intr
  wire imcr.lint0  -> lapic0.lint0
  wire lapic0.intr -> cpu0.intr
  wire lapic0.nmi  -> cpu0.nmi

  # The legacy lines land twice, on the 8259A input they have always had and on
  # the I/O APIC input the MP specification assigns them.
  wire com1.irq    -> pic1.ir4
  wire com1.irq    -> ioapic.irq4

  # IRQ0 and IRQ8, through the multiplexer the HPET's `LEG_RT_CNF` is. The
  # object block above says why; here is only the wiring. `legoff` inverts the
  # bit, so its output is high exactly while the two legacy chips own the two
  # lines.
  #
  # IRQ0 is the one line whose two halves do not carry the same number: it
  # reaches I/O APIC input **2** rather than 0, whichever chip is driving it,
  # and the MADT's interrupt source override is the other half of that.
  wire hpet0.legacy -> legoff.in

  wire legoff.out   -> pitgate.in0
  wire pit0.out0    -> pitgate.in1
  wire pitgate.out  -> pic1.ir0
  wire pitgate.out  -> ioapic.irq2

  wire legoff.out   -> rtcgate.in0
  wire cmos.irq     -> rtcgate.in1
  wire rtcgate.out  -> pic2.ir0
  wire rtcgate.out  -> ioapic.irq8

  # The other half of each multiplexer: the two comparators, driving the same
  # two nets. Two sources on one net is §4.3's wired-OR and it is the right
  # shape here — at most one side of a multiplexer is ever enabled, so the
  # resolve never has to arbitrate between them.
  wire hpet0.legacy -> hpetir0.in0
  wire hpet0.t0     -> hpetir0.in1
  wire hpetir0.out  -> pic1.ir0
  wire hpetir0.out  -> ioapic.irq2

  wire hpet0.legacy -> hpetir8.in0
  wire hpet0.t1     -> hpetir8.in1
  wire hpetir8.out  -> pic2.ir0
  wire hpetir8.out  -> ioapic.irq8

  # Comparators 0 and 1 on the inputs their route fields name, which they hold
  # only while the legacy replacement route is off. Comparator 2 is never
  # displaced, so it is wired straight through.
  wire legoff.out   -> hpetr0.in0
  wire hpet0.t0     -> hpetr0.in1
  wire hpetr0.out   -> ioapic.irq16

  wire legoff.out   -> hpetr1.in0
  wire hpet0.t1     -> hpetr1.in1
  wire hpetr1.out   -> ioapic.irq17

  wire hpet0.t2     -> ioapic.irq18

  # The SCI. `ACPI_CNTL[2:0]` picks one of five destinations and the bridge
  # drives one pin per destination, so all five are wired and at most one is
  # ever high.
  wire lpc.sci9    -> pic2.ir1
  wire lpc.sci9    -> ioapic.irq9
  wire lpc.sci10   -> pic2.ir2
  wire lpc.sci10   -> ioapic.irq10
  wire lpc.sci11   -> pic2.ir3
  wire lpc.sci11   -> ioapic.irq11
  wire lpc.sci20   -> ioapic.irq20
  wire lpc.sci21   -> ioapic.irq21

  # The `PIRQ` routers' outputs, each to the 8259A input it names and to the
  # I/O APIC input of the same number, because which one a given function ends
  # up on is a run-time decision. Wiring both is what makes the `_PRT` honest in
  # either mode: the global system interrupt and the ISA interrupt are the same
  # number, so one table describes both.
  #
  # The `PIRQ` *inputs* are not wired, and that is not an omission: the fabric
  # drives four of them itself, by rotating each function's `INTA#`-`INTD#` pin
  # by device number onto one of the bus's four interrupt nets, which this
  # bridge collects because it says `bus = "pci0"`. The NVMe controller's
  # interrupt reaches IRQ11 that way and by no line written here.
  wire lpc.irq3    -> pic1.ir3
  wire lpc.irq3    -> ioapic.irq3
  wire lpc.irq5    -> pic1.ir5
  wire lpc.irq5    -> ioapic.irq5
  wire lpc.irq6    -> pic1.ir6
  wire lpc.irq6    -> ioapic.irq6
  wire lpc.irq7    -> pic1.ir7
  wire lpc.irq7    -> ioapic.irq7
  wire lpc.irq10   -> pic2.ir2
  wire lpc.irq10   -> ioapic.irq10
  wire lpc.irq11   -> pic2.ir3
  wire lpc.irq11   -> ioapic.irq11
  wire lpc.irq12   -> pic2.ir4
  wire lpc.irq12   -> ioapic.irq12
  wire lpc.irq14   -> pic2.ir6
  wire lpc.irq14   -> ioapic.irq14
  wire lpc.irq15   -> pic2.ir7
  wire lpc.irq15   -> ioapic.irq15

  # `lpc.irq4` is absent because COM1 is already on that net and `lpc.irq9` is
  # absent because the SCI's default selection lands there; a second driver on
  # either would make two unrelated sources indistinguishable. The board's own
  # `pirq-routes` send all four nets to IRQ10 and IRQ11 anyway.

  # The timer's counter 2 and the board's port B — the calibration path.
  wire sysctl.gate2 -> pit0.gate2
  wire pit0.out2    -> sysctl.timer2
  wire pit0.out1    -> sysctl.refresh

  # Reset, from the chipset's fast port and from 0xcf9 through the north
  # bridge's pass-through. **A20 is deliberately not wired**, for the reason at
  # the top of this file.
  wire sysctl.reset -> cpu0.reset
}
